<!-- Part 14 e2e scenario 6/6: minimal, hand-reviewable example of Virtual
     Types being keyed by (owner, BrowseName), not by BrowseName alone; see
     virtual_type_explosion.md section 4 for the full TMCStateMachineType
     story this mirrors at a much smaller scale. The point: the SAME
     BrowseName ("CurrentState") is declared independently on three
     different real ObjectTypes, and each occurrence is a brand-new Variable
     declaration, so each needs its own Virtual Type, even though nothing
     is "overridden" anywhere and two of the three are byte-for-byte
     identical in shape (Optional would not change this; Variables always
     need a VT to carry their own ValueRank/Datatype, section 6). This is
     NOT an example of runaway Virtual Type growth: 3 real types, 5
     Instance Declarations, 3 Virtual Types is a completely ordinary,
     sub-1:1 ratio; see virtual_type_explosion.md's correction note for why
     an earlier draft of this scenario was mislabeled as one.
     Meanwhile SubStateA/SubStateB are plain, unmodified Object references
     to their nominal type with no local content of their own, so, per
     the need-based design, neither gets a Virtual Type at all.

     Shape:
       MiniMachineType
         CurrentState : PropertyType/String (Mandatory)      VT #1
         SubStateA     : SubMachineAType (Mandatory)          plain reference, NO VT
         SubStateB     : SubMachineBType (Mandatory)          plain reference, NO VT
       SubMachineAType
         CurrentState : PropertyType/String (Mandatory)      VT #2 (identical shape to VT #1!)
       SubMachineBType
         CurrentState : PropertyType/Int32 (Mandatory)       VT #3 (different Datatype)

     Expect exactly 3 real ObjectTypes, 5 raw Instance Declarations
     (CurrentState x3, SubStateA, SubStateB), and exactly 3 Virtual Types:
     one per (owner, "CurrentState") pair, not one per BrowseName. VT #1 and
     VT #2 are structurally identical (both PropertyType/String) yet must
     remain two separate classes: they are two independently-declared
     Instance Declarations that happen to look the same today, not the same
     declaration seen twice; merging them would be wrong the moment either
     one changes independently in a future spec revision. -->
